  "worked_solution": "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,3)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+3-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0,$ so $x=3$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(3)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-6(3)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
